Here is Astra 6.2. I had it formalize these gaps in Lean, so there is no question they are real.<p>Masullo’s proof of the Riemann hypothesis is invalid as written. On p. 495, a cutoff erases the very term the proof is trying to compute. On p. 29, a family of integrals that the proof needs to be uniformly bounded actually tends to infinity. Lean verified both mathematical obstructions.<p>1. The orbital cutoff erases the entire shell. Let Aₖ be the manuscript’s k-th set and αₖ its indicator. The sets shrink as k increases. The factor αₖ₊₁-αₖ therefore picks out the shell Aₖ∖ Aₖ₊₁, where it equals -1. In equation (11.3.2.3), the author multiplies by a later cutoff αⱼ in the same variable. Once j≥ k+1, that cutoff keeps only points in Aⱼ⊆ Aₖ₊₁. Every point of the shell has been removed. The integrand is then identically zero before integration, so its limit is zero as well. Manuscript, pp. 493–496 · Lean theorem<p>Why does that matter? The author uses this limit to recover a general orbital trace that can be positive. For example, over ℚ at the prime 2, take a nonnegative smooth test function of the idelic norm, supported in (3&#x2F;2,5&#x2F;2) and equal to 1 at norm 2. The claimed orbital sum has a positive term from the inverse uniformizer, whose norm is 2. An eventually zero sequence cannot recover that value. This invalidates the general trace calculation invoked on p. 520, although the large-prime terms needed on that page could still vanish for a separate reason. Orbital trace formula, p. 495 · Use in the global trace formula, p. 520<p>2. The bound needed for Montel’s theorem is false. Take ℚ, the trivial character, and z=3&#x2F;2. On 1&lt;t&lt;2, the manuscript’s nonnegative cutoffs satisfy ϕₙ(t)→ t^{-3&#x2F;2}. One positive contribution to the real-place integral therefore has limiting density
\[
\frac12\,\frac{t^{1&#x2F;2}\phi_n(t)}{t(t-1)}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\frac{1}{2t^2(t-1)}
\;\ge\;
\frac{1}{8(t-1)}.
\]
The last expression has infinite integral on (1,2). Each cutoff can have a finite integral because it vanishes near 1, but Fatou’s lemma shows that along every sequence approaching the prescribed limit, those integrals tend to infinity. Thus the claimed common bound fails even at the single point z=3&#x2F;2. No such sequence can produce the finite holomorphic limit asserted on p. 29. Manuscript, §§1.4.2–1.4.5 · Lean theorem<p>The second error alone breaks the deduction of RH. The manuscript uses that holomorphic limit to rule out poles of L&#x27;&#x2F;L in the region where it wants to exclude zeros of L. Since the limit fails, that exclusion has no proof here. The point z=3&#x2F;2 is a counterexample to the manuscript’s bound.
